41|0@sysadmin^1438696309#ctr2jrn:jmnugent$:In our environment,.. the Dept in question pays for User-breakage. We (IT Dept) will do what we can to cover "wear and tear",etc.... but any kind of obvious user-damage (even if it was an accident).. is almost always charged back to the Dept. It's up to that Dept's Manager to have a "sit-down talk" with the employee and get them to straighten up. (or get shipped-off). 
16|0@sysadmin^1438697094#ctr2yg1:ballr4lyf$:This is the best response, but really needs to go a step further. *ALL* IT purchases for the department should be charged to the department. You want to have a high end gaming PC to read emails and craft spreadsheets... I'm not going to argue with you. If you get it approved by your manager, I'll deploy our image to it and let you go to town. Lost your company cell phone, get your manager to approve procurement of a new one.
1|0@sysadmin^1438702977#ctr6h2r:ScannerBrightly$:&gt; I'll deploy our image to it and let you go to town. Does your image grow with drivers for every device you work with? Or a template with drivers injected at some point?
3|0@sysadmin^1438707141#ctr9alx:Cyphr$:Our company does one image per hardware configuration, with about 3 hardware configurations and 200 employees, it's not too bad to manage.
1|0@sysadmin^1438713868#ctre036:ScannerBrightly$:Sounds about right. The OP was talking about letting anyone get anything they can pass thru their manager, which seems like it would be 50 configs for 200 users.
5|0@sysadmin^1438709603#ctrb0ct:LOLBaltSS$:Easiest way to do it currently is to just have a generalized SysPrep image and then inject drivers during install. On BMC Client Management, I just have a central repo of drivers and when it images, it detects the hardware and puts it into it's own driver store which is referenced in the registry. I have one company image that I use with just about whatever I need to install it on. It's hardware agnostic.
5|0@sysadmin^1438697552#ctr3768:jmnugent$:Yeah.. generally speaking, that's how we work. Although because we standardize on DELL,.. we purchase in bulk.. and for the "general infrastructure/support" costs are rolled into the SLA's we sign with each Dept. So for "standard equipment" -- the costs come out of a "general fund". But you're right,.. if a Dept wants something that's technically "non-standard" (like higher-end machines, Apple gear, wall-mounted TV's, etc,etc).. they have to provide a Charge Code. 
1|0@sysadmin^1438702632#ctr695j:ScriptLife$:This is pretty much exactly how we did it at my last job, though we didn't really have to worry about costs, that was between IT purchasing and the various departments for non-standard items or special purchases. QA needs 50 iPads to test our software? No problem, IT Ops just accepts the delivery. Dev needs a rack of servers to run test labs? No problem, IT Ops architects or validates a solution/build, then receives it and sets it up once it arrives. The cost part is out of our purview. As for user-damaged gear, we were on a 2-3 year hardware cycle and anything outside of that cycle was charged to the department. Only exception was if it was manufacturer defect, or if we were just swapping it out for software issues.
