7|0@sysadmin^1438696991#ctr2whd:realslacker$:We make them pay, and users always get back their own laptop again. Drop your laptop and break the screen, replace screen and you still have a busted ass looking laptop because the plastics kit is damaged. The worse a user treats their laptop the worse their laptop looks and works. They pay for all damages, and if they return it in an un-usable condition we force them to buy it out rather than return it. I have one user who keeps breaking laptops beyond repair, if she goes through another one she's getting an early 2000's toughbook instead of a new laptop.
1|0@sysadmin^1438808857#ctsshpx:kilkor$:You're an IT director and that's how you think? That's a really despicable practice, and I'd hate to work for you.
1|0@sysadmin^1438809449#ctsswww:realslacker$:To which part are you referring... The part about making irresponsible employee's pay for damages incurred through negligence or the tongue-in-cheek bit about the toughbook? If it's the pay for damages part we came to this policy when laptop after laptop was destroyed, lost, or stolen. Now users sign an agreement when they get the laptop and they understand the policy. We are on a three year depreciation and replacement schedule, so they don't have to suffer a very long time if they bust up a laptop. When they turn it in, and it's too damaged they don't pay full retail price... they pay the expected resale price, and they get the laptop, usually around $100. We evaluate everything on a case by case basis, and we try to give users the benefit of the doubt. The alternative was to give the problem users new/working laptops when they break theirs and repair their laptops and hand them out to someone else. I don't think a user who has taken good care of their laptop and has a drive failure or something else should end up with a crap piece of equipment that someone else turned in. TL;DR - you treat your equipment like crap, you get crap equipment... don't see a problem with that policy
1|0@sysadmin^1438809887#ctst86n:kilkor$:What you call negligence, I'm assuming to me would be simple accidents. Maybe I'm wrong. If someone is on call 24/7/365 and has a company issued phone that they forget is in their pocket and jump into a pool, is that negligence to you and worthy of charging them the 2-300 bucks for a new phone, or is that a single case of an accident that you would overlook?
1|0@sysadmin^1438773372#cts6mbv:binarycow$:&gt; I have one user who keeps breaking laptops beyond repair, if she goes through another one she's getting an early 2000's toughbook instead of a new laptop. I like this idea. If we didn't have an 'approved models' list which contained only the past five years, I'd do that. I know of an office which has a computer that was purchased in 2001. It's in storage, but I know they still own it.
