3|0@sysadmin^1438701734#ctr5oc2:Zangypoo$:Occam's razor.
1|0@sysadmin^1438697555#ctr378o:thewunderbar$:This is not a IT problem. It is an HR problem. Honestly, you need to have an HR policy that states that damaged caused by the employee is the employee's responsibility. I think it's fine to give someone one free pass, but They won't start taking care of it until the cost burden becomes on them. Talk to your HR people, give them solid numbers to show how much money this is costing the company, including hardware, his lost time for not having a working machine, the time of your staff for dealing with him when it happens, etc.
6|0@sysadmin^1438698450#ctr3p6u:kilkor$:Nope, others have pointed this out already, but the charge needs to go back to his team/manager and come from their budget. No company should be holding an employee personally responsible for damage to a piece of equipment that has been deemed critical for their job role. This allows the manager to make decisions about the shitty employee that basically say "break another one and I'll have to let you go because the company can't afford to keep replacing your hardware" It completely removes any messiness that comes from employees replacing company hardware with their own money, then feeling entitled to keeping that property when they leave. 
1|0@sysadmin^1438774097#cts6u1v:binarycow$:&gt; No company should be holding an employee personally responsible for damage to a piece of equipment that has been deemed critical for their job role. Heh. In the military, they give us thousands of dollars of equipment that we store at home. Then, we sign for millions of dollars of equipment for work. We lose/break something? An investigation happens, and if neglect is found, we pay for it. Sometimes, if the dollar amount is small enough, or if its not a sensitive item, we just pay for it. Granted, us enlisted folk only lose half our pay for two months (so maximum of one month pay)... But officers, if neglect is found, no limit.
1|0@sysadmin^1438809133#ctssos7:kilkor$:I mean, I did say "company". I meant it in the business sense and not the military sense =P The military is just a money sink and is in no way a profit leading company.
2|0@sysadmin^1438717845#ctrgu3i:IT_dude_76$:It also prevents getting slapped with a lawsuit for what amounts to wage docking. In some states, you're going to have to compensate the employee for those expenses plus interest. In other states, you're going to have to compensate the employee *triple* the expenses, *triple* interest, plus extra punitive fees, plus hefty fines to the state. Saving $5000 by refusing to replace three laptops suddenly turns into $50,000 in expenses and fines.
2|0@sysadmin^1438698124#ctr3io6:AMidgetAndAClub$:We luckily haven't had much of this except from our wireless division. Those guys are rough and once for one reason or another dropped it from the top of a tower. I don't know. Don't ask... But phones, phones on the other hand have insurance. And we always provide a Lifeproof case or something else they "prefer" like an Otterbox. We had one tech randomly have a seizure, and smash his laptop screen. We just put a new screen in. 
1|0@sysadmin^1438773623#cts6oxz:binarycow$:&gt; We had one tech randomly have a seizure, and smash his laptop screen. We just put a new screen in. There's a difference between an accident and neglect.
