2|0@sysadmin^1438710259#ctrbgxy:Wargala$:I work at a construction company, and there's a choice. First break, ok, it happens. Second break, especially if done quickly after the first, their dept. can pay for it, or I can get out one of my "spares". Usually the "spares" are super old pieces of shit that barely work. You do that once, word gets around to take care of your stuff.
3|0@sysadmin^1438717186#ctrgcos:waldizzo$:Dump a can of soda on your laptop? "Somehow" get lotion all over your laptop keyboard? Let your kids beat the shit out of the cdrom tray? Run the laptop over with your car? No problem! Here have another one! Lose your HID door access card... That'll be 10 dollars for a replacement please. 
6|0@sysadmin^1438717252#ctrgehb:IT_dude_76$:&gt;There's no HR process for these cases so we have to just image a new one, hand it to him and joke about him getting a fisher price one next time! You answered your own question. Get HR to make a process. Without HR's support, there's no a lot that can be done. Charging users to replace hardware is basically illegal (I'm not going to argue this point, if you disagree then ask your lawyer and prepare for a surprise), and taking it away from them entirely may not be an option if their manager demands that they have a laptop for whatever reason. Your only real option is to tell them to be more careful every time you give them new hardware, and obviously that doesn't work too well. Ideally, HR would have a disciplinary process linked to hardware damage. First accident in 18 months is free, second in 18 months is a writeup, third in 18 months is suspension, etc. (Most HR departments try to leave out the time requirement, but wind up regretting it when they have to fire a 20-year veteran for damaging his laptop for the third time in his entire career.) EDIT: I forgot to mention, you should be issuing durable equipment. This sounds obvious, but some departments are less than intelligent in this regard. I've seen departments issue fragile phones, refuse to purchase cases, and then act surprised when they're shelling out cash to fix/replace shattered screens, etc. Cell phones? They come with an otterbox case. Sure, it costs $80, but it turns a fragile piece of expensive equipment into a fucking tank. Given the price of the cell phone, if the cases save even 1 replacement or repair per every 6 (ish) cell phones issued, it's paid for itself. The employee is expected to keep the phone inside the case except for cleaning or if it gets dropped into liquid or has something spilled on it (in which case, power it down, remove case immediately, take battery out, leave out to dry). Employee removes the case anyway and damages it? They get referred to HR for discipline. Laptops? They're either toughbooks or the "extra durable" line from your manufacturer of choice. They are issued with a padded bag. The laptop is to be transported only in that bag or a suitable alternative (e.g. backpack with padded laptop compartment). Employee decides to abandon the bag, drops laptop in the parking lot because they were carrying it without the bag? They get referred to HR for discipline. That brand new device with the fragile frame, scratchtastic screen, and buttons that have a tendency to fall off? It's not being issued. The CEO or super-high-level executives want one? Fine, they can have one because I'm not going to waste my time arguing against something they're going to order me to give him anyway, but no one else.
2|0@sysadmin^1438726087#ctrmf4f:Fireproofjeans$:Seriously, give him a fisher price one and video tape it. Then bill his department for a replacement. Post the vid here so we can all have a giggle.
1|0@sysadmin^1438726198#ctrmhl4:girlgerms$:This isn't a technical issue, it's a HR issue. At my last place of work, we had the same thing - so we stumped up the cash for accidental damage protection on every single piece of equipment we bought: laptops, desktops, phones, tablets, peripherals. Means that we can just call up, get a tech out, and get it replaced. Trust me, it's worth the extra cash...
1|0@sysadmin^1438747502#ctryn36:irwincur$:Accidental damage warranty add on. Usually not all that expensive and it can actually be worth it for laptops - especially sales people who tend to like to use them in the car. One hard stop and it is done.
